WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Genetic conflicts between viruses and hosts shape rapid evolution across species. This is Planetarian Daily, episode 223. Mechanical signals in bone, an ancient virus in chimpanzee neurons, and protein pillars that hold mitochondria in shape. The malloc lab at the Fred Hutchinson Cancer Center, directed by H.H.M.I. investigator, Harmon Malik, tracks conflicts inside genomes that play out over a few generations, rather than thousands of years, in a report carried by Fizz.com.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:30.420
Org.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.800
Postdoc to Manash Bhattacharya found an opal stop codon inside alpha viruses, including

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.240
Sinbis and Chickengunya that sets a kinetic Goldilocks balance for polyprotein production.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.720
The virus can make sufficient protein to copy itself and still slip past immune surveillance

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.200
in people and in mosquitoes.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.880
Mosquitos do not need that same balance because their temperatures are lower, yet the codon

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.920
remains since it still lets the virus build hidden replication compartments.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:04.760
Graduate student Jeremy Hollis combined remote homology detection, alpha-fold structural

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:10.580
modeling, and functional genetics to show that HJURP, a protein that marks center mirrors

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.700
at cell division, is present in most animals even though it changes too fast for ordinary

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:16.920
search tools to catch.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:24.140
Former postdoc Ching Ho Chang found that the fruit fly Proudamine MS-77F, evolves quickly,

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:29.620
Suppressing a meodic drive that destroys X-bearing sperm and tilts brewed towards sons.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:37.240
Two papers and science advances lay out these mechanisms and supply rules that could guide development of new antiviral strategies.

00:01:38.223 --> 00:01:43.303
Dynamic force applied to the Tibia activated the Pieso-1 Mechanosensor Receptor

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:47.563
and produced measurable extensions in both health span and lifespan in mice.

00:01:47.563 --> 00:01:54.083
Traded mice, in work published in cell metabolism, showed reduced systemic inflammation and decreased

00:01:54.083 --> 00:01:56.203
immunosenscence within bone marrow.

00:01:56.863 --> 00:02:01.863
Genetic deletion of piezo1 eliminated those benefits and reversed the protective effects.

00:02:02.483 --> 00:02:07.103
Bone tissue here acts as an active regulator of whole body aging through its ability to

00:02:07.103 --> 00:02:09.263
sense and transduce mechanical signals.

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:14.667
Most people assumed the human brain became larger and more connected only through mutations

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:19.527
that rewrote protein coding genes in our lineage after its split from chimpanzees.

00:02:20.027 --> 00:02:28.367
A retroviral infection roughly 4 million years ago left DNA that still influences neuron growth and wiring in one lineage, but not the other.

00:02:29.007 --> 00:02:36.667
Once a retrovirus inserts itself into germ cell DNA, every descendant that inherits that stretch of sequence keeps it and hands it on.

00:02:37.227 --> 00:02:43.847
In chimpanzees that leftover viral stretch sits beside a gene young neurons used to grow out processes and make connections.

00:02:43.847 --> 00:02:48.447
The viral DNA holds that gene off during key developmental windows.

00:02:48.787 --> 00:02:53.867
Human ancestors either never took on that insertion or changed nearby sequence enough to stop

00:02:53.867 --> 00:02:56.287
the silencing, so the gene stays on.

00:02:56.907 --> 00:03:01.987
Lund University scientists used CRISPR to cut the viral segment out of chimpanzee stem cells.

00:03:02.487 --> 00:03:07.227
Once the segment was gone, the gene switched back on, and neurons in the mini-brain models

00:03:07.227 --> 00:03:09.247
grew farther and connected more readily.

00:03:09.827 --> 00:03:12.247
That cut cannot be tested in living animals.

00:03:12.247 --> 00:03:16.747
cells, so how much this regulatory difference shapes adult brain size or circuit layout

00:03:16.747 --> 00:03:17.627
is still unknown.

00:03:18.247 --> 00:03:22.207
The cell level effect does show up consistently in the culture systems they used.

00:03:23.098 --> 00:03:28.498
Protein pillars that stretch across the mitochondrial intermembrane space hold the geometry oxidative

00:03:28.498 --> 00:03:30.578
phosphorylation needs to run efficiently.

00:03:31.178 --> 00:03:36.038
A mapping of those structures between the inner and outer membranes, carried by Fizz.org,

00:03:36.538 --> 00:03:38.498
shows they maintain that precise spacing.

00:03:38.498 --> 00:03:44.058
Disruption of the pillars alters Christae organization and reduces cellular energy output.

00:03:44.378 --> 00:03:49.198
The mapping fills in a layer of mitochondrial organization that had been underappreciated.

00:03:49.458 --> 00:03:54.327
University of Helsinki researchers compared species occurrence and abundance records

00:03:54.327 --> 00:03:59.267
against climate variables and against habitat metrics that included natural state and the

00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:01.527
share of land modified by human activity.

00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:06.727
Habitat-related factors accounted for more variants in both presence and abundance across

00:04:06.727 --> 00:04:12.067
the studied animals than climate data alone. Local land management decisions provide a more

00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:17.287
immediate and actionable lever for biodiversity conservation than broad-scale climate projections

00:04:17.287 --> 00:04:23.587
in this system. Solutions to mitigate biodiversity loss depend more directly on habitat quality than

00:04:23.587 --> 00:04:30.563
on temperature or precipitation trends here. In Ayang Gei, Ecuador, the MCBL project trained children

00:04:30.563 --> 00:04:37.343
ages 7 to 12 to run monthly low tide surveys over 1,500 square meters of fixed transects.

00:04:37.763 --> 00:04:44.423
Collectors built from reuse PVC material gathered 1,198 cigarette butts over 18 months of

00:04:44.423 --> 00:04:45.943
operation along the boardwalk.

00:04:46.603 --> 00:04:50.903
An exchange program that let residents and visitors trade collected waste for sports

00:04:50.903 --> 00:04:57.923
equipment such as snorkels or balls removed an additional 941 butts plus 97.9 kilograms

00:04:57.923 --> 00:04:59.983
of other trash across eight events.

00:05:00.523 --> 00:05:03.343
The total number of butts collected directly from the sand

00:05:03.343 --> 00:05:07.703
declined 53.2% between 2023 and 2024.

00:05:08.603 --> 00:05:11.663
Data went into open repositories, including mentally data,

00:05:12.043 --> 00:05:15.423
and 85.7% of participants shared their findings

00:05:15.423 --> 00:05:17.223
with families and social circles,

00:05:17.523 --> 00:05:20.363
which prompted the local cabildo to fold the program

00:05:20.363 --> 00:05:22.583
